ndations and Secretary  of the Board of Governors of the  National Press Club I'm going to skip  over our speakers for the moment go to  Clayton voice deputy news editor  knight-ridder tribune newswire and vice  president of the National Press Club  John Peterson chairman of the National  Press Club speakers committee Harry  Stouffer the Pittsburgh post-gazette and  chairman of the awards committee Charles  Alexander the director of the Medill  News Service in Washington DC  Leland Schwartz the publisher of States  News Service  and James McTigue Washington bureau  chief of the American Banker today we're  welcoming to the National Press Club two  of the most honored reporters of our day  Donald Barletta and James Steele they  have worked together as a team of  investigative reporters for the  Philadelphia Inquirer since 1971 their  specialty is researching analyzing and  writing in-depth series of stories about  complex issues and institutions that  profoundly affect American lives they  have won virtually every major national  journalism award for their investigative  reports including two Pulitzer Prizes  for national reporting their first  Pulitzer in 1975 was for a series of  articles called auditing the IRS which  detailed the inequities of an Internal  Revenue Service audits their second  Pulitzer in 1989 was for a series titled  the great tax giveaway in 7 installments  they told how members of Congress had  salted hundreds of secret tax breaks for  favored constituents into the Tax Reform  Act of 1986 in his book the new  muckrakers  Leonard Downey jr. said the journalism  of Barlett and Steele quote represents a  significant step beyond traditional  muckraking instead of just reporting  still unproven allegations and focusing  on individual corruption they reveal  with expert analysis and thorough  documentation what has systematically  gone wrong with the powerful complex  institutions that affect so much of life  today it is said that they follow the  maxim of the Great Wall Street Journal  editor Barney Kilgore that for every  inch of copy in one of their stories  there be an inch or more thick  of supporting documentation back in the  office and their cramped office has no  clear space anywhere according to an  Inquirer colleague who calls it quote an  organized mess they are here to talk  about their last published work a series  of stories titled America what went  wrong the series concluded that the  public's perception that something was  wrong with the American economy was  right that unbridled greed on Wall  Street and deal-making here in  Washington was destroying industries  that million of millions of people  relied upon for their livelihoods after  its publication last October there were  400 thousand requests for reprints of  the series and an expanded version of  the series was published in softcover  this year two highly favorable reviews  the Los Angeles Times said this  important and profoundly disturbing book  should be required reading for voters in  the upcoming elections and this just in  an AP story on what Ross Perot reads he  gives it rave reviews he said I read it  from the Philadelphia paper the first  time and it is world-class everybody  should read it Perot said I hope that  nobody that reads it can live with it  he also said ladies and gentlemen please  welcome Don varlet and Jim Steele  I don't know how to respond to that one  of the questions that we were all we've  been asked quite frequently is how the  series began and it was published about  two weeks before the election last  November and we received a few letters  and a few phone calls afterwards  questioning whether or not the Enquirer  had engaged in a conspiracy to secure  the election of Harris Wofford  and obviously anybody who's ever been in  the Enquirer newsroom knows that the  terms conspiracy and Inquirer newsroom  are mutually exclusive that's not  possible  but we had a number of these questions  and finally we told one person yes  you're right it was a conspiracy it  began in August of 1989 when we wanted  to see gene Roberts who was in the  editor The Enquirer and we said look  you're not going to believe this but we  have it on good authority that in April  of 1991 senator Heinz is going to die in  a helicopter plane crash and dick  thornburgh is going to resign his  Attorney General and run for Senator  Heinz's seat that November and this guy  named Harris Wofford is going to run  against him and nobody knows him and we  want to make sure he wins the election  so we'd like to spend the next 20 months  working on next two years working on  this and Robert said sure go ahead take  whatever time you need it's no problem  that wasn't really how it began but if  that satisfies conspiracists okay it  really began like everything we've ever  done has begun with a simple single  question what was happening to people  who worked for companies that underwent  restructuring and with that question  we'd set out across the country  beginning in late 89 and continuing  through 90 Jim would go to a Martell  California interview a sawmill worker  I'd interview a department store clerk  in Charleston West Virginia  he didn't review someone who worked for  a meatpacking meat processing plant in  Sedalia Missouri and I didn't  view middle level manager in Niles  Michigan at a manufacturing plant this  went on and on and every time we came  back and transcribed the notes we saw  something we had never seen before in  all the years in this business the  interviews were identical  it didn't matter whether the person was  white collar or blue collar white black  or Hispanic high school dropout  college-educated it made no difference  the responses were generally the same  people were being forced into ever lower  paying jobs they were losing all or part  of their healthcare benefits they were  forced to pay for a larger percentage of  their what health care for those people  who kept it they were losing all or part  of their pensions or their future  retirement benefits were in question at  the same time the interviews were going  on were doing a lot of statistical work  and we and we picked up very quickly  that the statistics were showing that  these weren't aberrations that we just  hadn't happened to settle on the right  people in two dozen states in two  thousand different cities and that these  were more than anecdotes but they really  fell into a broad pattern as to what was  happening in the economy we have to  point out it obviously there are those  people who disagree with with some of  the conclusions we've drawn from the  statistics one of the kind of fun things  we've had if over the years of working  together and because so much of what we  do is Washington based we're thinking of  publishing a collection of great  Washington letters every time we write a  series people out there respond not only  in Philadelphia but all across the  country they'll write a letter to their  member of Congress or they'll write a  letter to the White House and they'll  get a response and they thoughtfully  send all the correspondence along to us  and it's going to make a view this is a  future retirement plan it's going to  make a great multi volume collection of  letters one of the packages we got in  the mail just last week was a 100 page  plus internal White House memorandum  prepared on America what went wrong in  December of 1991 in which the White  House staff went through everything in  this series and analyzed it and it was a  I will read a whole hundred pages but  there was just one graph that kind of  jumped out which was interesting it said  while there are many allegations in the  series that are factually wrong there  are others ie the special favors that  Congress grants to itself such as  inflation adjusted pensions with which  we would agree  it seems that basically when you go  through the hundred pages those those  points that are raised in the series in  the book with which the White House  agreed were all accurate and those  points with which they didn't agree were  statistically wrong it was somewhat like  a small group of people who said that  the middle class really isn't sure is  shrinking but that's because of all the  upward mobility and they pointed to the  fact that there are more millionaires  than ever now and they're absolutely  right we went back and looked at the tax  returns for 1980 to 1989 and the number  of returns filed by families and  individuals with incomes of more than a  million dollars went up from four  thousand four hundred and fourteen to  sixty 1987 or an increase of thirteen  hundred and four percent which is a  clearly an indication of everyone  becoming a millionaire except if you  look at it in a different way and you  see that the increase in terms of total  returns filed went from point zero zero  four seven two point zero five five two  which means in 187 years if everyone  hangs in there a full 1% of the tax  returns will be filed by millionaires  and if you can hang in for nineteen  hundred years it'll be ten percent but  that's not an inflation adjusted dollars  and unfortunately with the exception of  our first series which was published in  1971 The Inquirer has reprinted  everything we've ever done in a tabloid  format and in the request for those  reprints generally have ranged between  twenty and fifty thousand traditionally  the reprints are delayed about three to  four weeks after the completion of the  series  but this time ex-king the new editor  decided he wanted the reprints available  the day the series ended and so the  reprint was literally tagged onto the  final press run of part 9 and they ran  off 18,000 copies and on with a little  box that day in the final installment  saying the reprints were available in  the Inquirer Lobby the next day we got a  call from the security people saying you  might want to come down and look because  they had to bring in extra security  people just to control the crowds not  because they were unruly or anything  like that because there were so many  people trying to get into the building  we took that where the green assault  went down and never went out the front  door for the next two weeks the 18,000  were gone in two days and I think the  statistics now are fairly well known  that there overall there were 400,000  distributed and we received well over  20,000 letters from all but about two or  three states what is not so well known  as this and this is very important for  you reporters who might want to go back  and talk to editors who might be  frustrated marketing executives menu  masquerading as editors that the  circulation of the paper for nine days  went up every single day as many as  10,000 a day at the end of the series it  dropped back down but we did receive  many letters from people who said they  had not subscribed the paper before but  did so now and letters from a couple  that said we bought three copies each  day and gave the two other copies of the  series to friends there was one major  disappointment from this series of Bill  Moyers did two one-hour programs based  on an adaptation this reason when we  were going through this process we  thought wow this is it this is a big  meal ticket to the really good money of  the electronic media we're going to be  out of here  and everything was really going well  until the day the first program aired  and now we know what it's like to be  savaged by the news media and it was bad  enough we were savaged but their own  paper The Enquirer TV critic reviewed it  and said everybody should watch these  programs that are really great and then  he did this truly malicious thing and I  want to read this so get the words  exactly right he says because we got to  remember now we forget we're going to be  the Siskel and Ebert of economic news he  says he says on the TV personality scale  Bartlett and steel are so laid back is  to be almost completely horizontal and  then I mean we never did anything to  this guy as far as I know but then he  got really personal he says at one point  Bartlett gets so outraged that he  actually arches an eyebrow and then he  concluded by saying the two of them are  Clarke Kennish enough to make Bill  Moyers look like Morton Downey jr. and  at that point we sent a letter to a  Moyers asking if you'd like to join in  litigation but one of our lawyer friends  pointed out the truth is still an  absolute defense so wised against a  gentle talk a little bit more about the  response  want to talk a little bit about the  reaction to the series and now the  subsequent reaction now that the book is  out there because it we think it says a  lot for our profession and also has a  great deal to say about the American  public as well don alluded to these  twenty thousand letters that we received  there was a certain quality to the  response both in the letters and in the  phone calls that we received right after  the original series he had an  astonishing volume of this mail began  coming in phone lines were jamming The  Inquirer switchboard some of the letters  and requests came in by the most bizarre  manner some were written on the back of  wine labels from the Napa Valley in  California one request for reprints was  scribbled on the back of the marching  song on Wisconsin one day after the  series originally appeared a frantic  caller came called from the fella  Manning the switchboard thinks it was  somewhere in the Midwest and said he had  heard by word of mouth that there was  this series that The Inquirer had done  on the American economy by two fellows  by the name of Bartles and Jaymes  this good natured flirt later sent him a  copy of the reprint and he says he threw  in a 6-pack of a peach wine cooler as  well wherever that gentleman is we will  thank him for his support you know what  what accounts for this reaction I mean  we keep being asked that and we ask  ourselves that question and the most I  think obvious thing that we've concluded  the others have actually concluded is  that this has hit a chord among a great  number of American people not just  blue-collar people not just white color  people not just people who have been  hurt in the last generation but many  who've actually benefited from some of  the rules changes but who are now  concerned for one reason or another  about the direction of the country the  interesting thing which I want to speak  to just briefly today is that the reason  this particular subject has hit a chord  and that it did so in by using  techniques and processes a journalism  that supposedly nobody wants to read  anymore this thing was long 73 thousand  words in the original form it's now  considerably longer in the book form it  was filled with numbers it dealt with  the economy taxation fiscal policy the  federal deficit all of these things that  supposedly we are over and over again  that readers don't want to read about  but not only did America what went wrong  contain an abundance of this kind of  information but in these 20 thousand  letters and in these scores and scores  of phone calls we got people pointed  precisely to those numbers those  statistics as giving what one called the  hard facts as to what has happened in  the country this was from an owner of a  small business in Pennsylvania who wrote  us you have articulated what so many of  us have had a gut feeling about but lack  the statistics to prove a Miami woman  wrote this is what newspapers are all  about information helping to explain  what's going on in our lives and part as  and as part of the larger picture and  research  statistics not just hearsay and historia  Oregon man after praising the paper for  both the scope of the enterprise and  also particularly for the numbers it  included said very simply this is what  journalism needs to be doing yet for all  of this the notion seems to exist in our  business that people don't want to read  about these things they're not  interested in numbers they're not  interested in depth and we to this day  remain baffled by that particular notion  we can't figure out why it is so complex  is it so complicated that people do not  understand that after tax reform in 1986  those earning a million or more received  on average a tax cut of two hundred  eighty one thousand while those making  forty to fifty received on average a tax  cut of four hundred dollars why is that  so complicated why is it so complicated  to explain to people that manufacturing  jobs increased in the 50s the 60s and  the 70s the total number decreased in  the 1980s why is that so so complicated  we believe people are capable of  absorbing numbers information that sort  but you can't just spew it out you have  to put it in some kind of a context we  heard bill greider talk about this very  eloquently just a couple of weeks ago  and he makes the point that so much of  the press fails to bring its message  into focus because it doesn't bring the  subject into perspective and and he  fails as he put it to provide a context  for what we tell readers and one that at  least gives them some plausible  connection to their own lives we see  examples of this all around us all the  time recently we've seen this debate  over the balanced budget amendment and  people are made quite a big issue  writing about this and reporting it but  few if any of these stories make the  point that this whole debate is in  effect the fifth time we've been dealing  with this issue since 1974 there have  been four previous measures enacted to  deal with a balanced budget and since  that time there have been 18 consecutive  budget deficits ten of them record  breakers obviously we  from a legislative standpoint have  become more preoccupied with passing  measures that supposedly will balance  the budget than with ballast and  balancing the budget well we've always  believed that people could absorb  numbers and listen to hard facts and  respond to them frankly we never had the  documentary evidence like we did as a  result of this series with these 20,000  letters and these letters are an  astonishing outpouring from the American  people all over the country all walks of  life rich poor and as Don said black  white Hispanic no simple way to describe  them they show very much the hopes the  dreams the concerns that so many people  have for the country and I think most  interesting of all to us their optimism  and in closing I'd like to read you just  the comments of one woman from  Pennsylvania who wrote us about America  what went wrong and what she said was  very symbolic of what literally hundreds  of others wrote us after thanking the  newspaper for investing the time and the  effort she said quite simply there are a  lot of good people who care and who want  to do something to turn this situation  around most of them are mute you to have  opened a Pandora's box of trouble and  grief that the heavies have been sitting  on for a long time  but I think you have pinched the  conscience of the silent majority and  hopefully something very positive will  result thank you so much we'd be happy  to answer some questions that you may  have  thank you for those remarks a questioner  wants to know what is the state of  investigative reporting today 20 years  after Watergate this is a question we  are constantly especially from younger  people coming into the business because  they get a lot of gloom and doom out  there and since I'm older I can tell  people that the gloom and doom is not  warranted the reason there is a there's  a sense that investigative reporting is  not as as great or as prevalent as it  once was is based on what people are  looking at in metropolitan newspapers  and metropolitan newspapers have never  ever been a gauge of the state of  investigative reporting in this country  there is probably not probably there is  more investigative reporting on medium  size and small daily newspapers going on  in this country now and at any time in  this nation's history and if you attend  a convention like Ayari or other  conventions where you see this work the  sad fact is that this these projects are  never pulled together in one place where  we can see them we never know about them  until you go out to a convention or or  hear someone someone call you and send  you one the volume of this work and the  quality of it is mind-boggling  and if it really should put most  metropolitan newspapers to shame they  should be embarrassed by it because the  observations that are being made on the  on the health of investigative reporting  really are based on what metropolitan  newspapers are doing but they've never  they never historically did that much to  begin with  so we think that basically the future is  very very bright  what specific training do you recommend  for young reporters for example  accounting or research methods one of  the nice things about doing something  like this you never know what kind of  questions you're going to be hit by  there's this is an interesting debate in  our profession about specialized  training and while I don't think either  one of us would ever discourage any type  of specialized training in a way we can  almost go too far in certain areas but I  think for young reporters I think Don  and I are both pretty much in agreement  on this rather than trying to foresee  and conceive your specialty early on you  should still get a broad-based training  in your field whether it's radio TV the  print media because only by seeing how  government works at all its levels to  get a full appreciation of how our  system as a whole operates the two of us  spent years laboring and what a famous  composer once described as the galleys  referring to all of it that hard work  you do day in and day out to churn out  stories we both spent years and years  doing daily stories of a wide variety on  a wide variety of subjects had nothing  to do with long-range reporting had  nothing to do with enterprise reporting  and I am most thankful for all the time  I spent doing that because I learned how  local school boards operate I've seen  how local City Councils operate I've  seen how what local political races are  like at the grassroots level you see and  absorb an appreciation of democracy  that's invaluable to your work at  whatever level you may pursue it so we  are firm believers and starting at small  medium sized papers definitely not at  large institutions if possible  we have two questions on the same theme  now that you know what went wrong to you  since the nation is turning the  situation around and your book documents  tremendous job losses due to mergers and  acquisitions and deregulation is this  still happening and where let me try  there let's see these are the book  documents tremendous job losses these  are continuing to happen I mean in  deregulation alone when Trans World  Airlines and America West and  Continental all entered bankruptcy some  time back everybody assumed that they  would all emerge at some point the  stories are becoming increasingly gloomy  for those airlines if not emerging at  least in some reduced fashion from what  they used to be what we see we see a  continuing fallout through the 1990s  largely because of some of the excesses  that were built up in the 1980s when you  look at the size of the corporate debt  loads that so many companies took on in  the 1980s we're going to be paying that  price for a long time it's even it's  hitting companies that otherwise are  very solid companies Macy's is probably  an excellent example of that Macy's is  basically from Olin what anyone can see  a very solid company but because of the  excessive debt that it took on in the  1980s as part of that gogo philosophy as  to why debt was good and made companies  cleaner and leaner and meaner and so  forth Macy's is now in bankruptcy and  starting to close stores starting to lay  off people so we see a continuing  weakness throughout the 90s one of the  interesting statistics that's in the  book that absolutely stunned us before  1994 1980 there were two bankruptcies in  the u.s. two corporate bankruptcies  where the companies had assets of two of  one billion or more since 1985 alone  there had been more than four dozen  bankruptcies for the  companies had assets of more than 1  billion or more most of those just in  recent years is a two-page chart in the  book on these bankruptcies the books  been out two months the charts already  out of date because there have been at  least two more billion dollar-plus  bankruptcies since then so that's going  to continue unfortunately to happen just  add one other thing to that and that's  that we talked about that all through  the 80s the the explanation you heard  for what was happening was we were  increasing shareholder value the 90s and  you see it every day in the newspaper  when you pick it up it's the global  economy and that there is very little  difference between the results of those  two phrases but the consequence is  growing out of those two phrases they  really mean lost jobs and for reporters  we hardly recommend that when somebody  tells you that moving a plant overseas  is going to create X number of jobs in  this country that a year later you go  back and say where precisely are those  jobs if they were created you can find  them if anybody wants to bother to look  do you sense that the nation is turning  the situation around and have you come  up with a series or other solutions for  the plight of the middle class which  seems to be shrinking as a result of  what you've described this is the the  one question we get all the time and I  think our answer has been very  consistent we're short term passiveness  and long term optimist the short term  pessimism has to do with the probability  that things are going to have to get  much worse before they get better  that may not be true but it tends to  look that way on the other hand I think  we're both very optimistic about the  American people and you can't help when  you spend as much time as we did going  around the country and one of the fun  things and the fascinating things not  necessarily fun but fascinating and very  instructive things from the book is  we've done about no more than 75 radio  talk shows now and half the states in  the country technology is such that  unfortunately we never have to leave the  Inquirer to do this we just sit in the  office rather than getting to travel but  the questions that come in in these I  mean we've been in the business long  enough as you people many of you people  have you can sort out the whack jobs of  very very quickly you know when the  questioner is a serious person and you  know when you're dealing with someone  who is really very concerned and this is  across the board it doesn't matter  whether it's a small town as we did a  radio station in Streeter Illinois that  had the mayor on along with the talk  show host or or Michael Jackson in Los  Angeles or or any of the programs in  Boston in New York the questions that  are coming in there are very similar to  what we found when we went out and did  the interviews around the country that  produced a series people are concerned  and they want to see something done and  clearly what's happening now is they're  casting about trying to find out what  the best way is to do that  I want to preface this story with a this  question with a story that mr. bralette  told me he said that his editors don't  like to ask them any questions about  their their work because they'll say  that's a very good question  and they'll come back two days later and  the story will be 90 inches longer the  question is did your editors cut any  good stuff  there's a funny story that max King  tells about this it's probably the best  one and that is in the original series  was picked up by roughly 50 newspapers  around the country at least that we know  of some picked up one part some picked  up two or three parts some ran actually  all nine parts but reduce them somewhat  and at one gathering we once we once  made the point that none of them of  course ran the series in entirety to  which max jumped up and said nobody ran  it in entirety including the  Philadelphia Enquirer like all of us  there were many good parts that  obviously you have to go through a  synthesis process and some of this  actually found their way back into the  book we also received many wonderful  tips from people around the country some  wonderful ideas about things to expand  on that we were unaware of so the book  also served as a mini follow-up to some  parts of the series so yes there's  always a synthesis process that that  goes through but nothing of real great  substance that in any way reduced the  impact or the significance of what we  felt the story was about was was left  out  the one questioner wants to know to what  degree do you connect the rise of Ross  Pro to your series or maybe another way  of asking this is are you seeing your  work recycled in the rhetoric of the  political campaign this year the Ross  Perot wins an interesting when sitting  in the office a few weeks after the  series ran and the phone rings and I  pick it up and he says I say Bartlett  and he says Don he said this is Ross  Perot and I said right  figured it was some friend but it really  was him and it's clear now when you look  back all those months what was happening  at the time because he said what he  wanted to do with the reprints was put  them into a restaurant that he goes to  every day not far from his home or a  couple of days a week which is basically  a restaurant that attracts a solid  middle-class group of people blue collar  white collar and he wanted to gauge the  reaction of the people who read the  reprint in the restaurant that was back  in November I don't think there's any  question you see large parts of it  coming out I think one of the we haven't  seen Governor Clinton's the full  proposal but just what we read in the  newspaper the tax part of it clearly  comes right out of the book when he's  talking about foreign corporations not  paying a share of taxes that they should  pay in this country and when he talks  about raising taxes at the top end of  the scale most of the tax policy issues  look like they came out of the book and  and other issues as well one last thing  I might add part of the appeal he's  having too is clearly a part of a  widespread disenchantment that we're  seeing around the country I'm sure many  of you are as well with both both  parties and there's no doubt that that  has to do with a lot of why he's getting  response he is people are so fed up  rightly or wrongly with what they  perceived to be the way both parties  have handled economic issues in  and the way government is operated in  Washington that's probably the single  most widespread reason for a lot of this  appeal a questioner asked in this age of  databases and power journalism what  resources did you rely on for your  information a library a computer your  car good question we used a lot of  databases electronic databases and we  have for years on a whole wide range of  things we used an electronic database  several years ago to decode one of the  special tax breaks and the tax reform  activator that was proposed after the  tax reform act of 86  so databases represent a wonderful  resource out there but most the most  significant probably database we used  was one we created ourselves rather than  buying material from somebody else we  simply one of the most exciting things  we do is to input data on a computer or  PC and all the charts in the book and  the series before that which would  capture the attention of so many people  were based on basically a very tedious  painstaking process I give an example 1  shows salaries and wages of people by  income groups in the 1980s for example  it's a very dramatic chart that shows  the increase of people earning more than  1 million what their cumulative  increases were versus people 20 to  50,000 the heart of the middle-class  very small increase that's based on ten  years of statistics of income from the  Internal Revenue Service or each one of  those reports is xeroxed or obtained  from the IRS and the particular numbers  input in a computer and then analyzed  some of the data we went back to the 30s  and 40s analyzing corporate payments and  tax collections each one input a  year-by-year  exciting stuff right  but tedious work but absolutely at the  heart of what you ultimately end up with  which is a very dramatic chart and  example so the computer in that sense  has become a tremendously important tool  for us other than that our techniques by  which we organize material and store it  and keep track of it are basically very  old-fashioned we don't have  sophisticated filing systems we file  information simply by subject matter  company individual general subject area  the single most valuable tool we know of  is the oldest tool that exists in  civilization and that is to reread the  material how many times do you  transcribe an interview or watch  something you've seen before and there's  something new in it the same thing is  true of a written document a written  report you see that over and over again  the mind can only focus on so many  things so we try to basically read and  reread our own material whether it's  transcribed interviews government  reports findings from databases and that  is served us better than any single  technique over the years is there an  advantage to having a dual investigative  team I think the the advantage is  obviously going to do twice as much work  the other real advantage and we see this  a lot of times with people working on  their first project or two is when two  people are working together you can  bounce things off 