xim of the Great Wall Street Journal  editor Barney Kilgore that for every  inch of copy in one of their stories  there be an inch or more thick  of supporting documentation back in the  office and their cramped office has no  clear space anywhere according to an  Inquirer colleague who calls it quote an  organized mess they are here to talk  about their last published work a series  of stories titled America what went  wrong the series concluded that the  public's perception that something was  wrong with the American economy was  right that unbridled greed on Wall  Street and deal-making here in  Washington was destroying industries  that million of millions of people  relied upon for their livelihoods after  its publication last October there were  400 thousand requests for reprints of  the series and an expanded version of  the series was published in softcover  this year two highly favorable reviews  the Los Angeles Times said this  important and profoundly disturbing book  should be required reading for voters in  the upcoming elections and this just in  an AP story on what Ross Perot reads he  gives it rave reviews he said I read it  from the Philadelphia paper the first  time and it is world-class everybody  should read it Perot said I hope that  nobody that reads it can live with it  he also said ladies and gentlemen please  welcome Don varlet and Jim Steele  I don't know how to respond to that one  of the questions that we were all we've  been asked quite frequently is how the  series began and it was published about  two weeks before the election last  November and we received a few letters  and a few phone calls afterwards  questioning whether or not the Enquirer  had engaged in a conspiracy to secure  the election of Harris Wofford  and obviously anybody who's ever been in  the Enquirer newsroom knows that the  terms conspiracy and Inquirer newsroom  are mutually exclusive that's not  possible  but we had a number of these questions  and finally we told