e losing all or part  of their pensions or their future  retirement benefits were in question at  the same time the interviews were going  on were doing a lot of statistical work  and we and we picked up very quickly  that the statistics were showing that  these weren't aberrations that we just  hadn't happened to settle on the right  people in two dozen states in two  thousand different cities and that these  were more than anecdotes but they really  fell into a broad pattern as to what was  happening in the economy we have to  point out it obviously there are those  people who disagree with with some of  the conclusions we've drawn from the  statistics one of the kind of fun things  we've had if over the years of working  together and because so much of what we  do is Washington based we're thinking of  publishing a collection of great  Washington letters every time we write a  series people out there respond not only  in Philadelphia but all across the  country they'll write a letter to their  member of Congress or they'll write a  letter to the White House and they'll  get a response and they thoughtfully  send all the correspondence along to us  and it's going to make a view this is a  future retirement plan it's going to  make a great multi volume collection of  letters one of the packages we got in  the mail just last week was a 100 page  plus internal White House memorandum  prepared on America what went wrong in  December of 1991 in which the White  House staff went through everything in  this series and analyzed it and it was a  I will read a whole hundred pages but  there was just one graph that kind of  jumped out which was interesting it said  while there are many allegations in the  series that are factually wrong there  are others ie the special favors that  Congress grants to itself such as  inflation adjusted pensions with which  we would agree  it seems that basically when you go  through the hundred pages those those  points that are raised in the