ng in extra security  people just to control the crowds not  because they were unruly or anything  like that because there were so many  people trying to get into the building  we took that where the green assault  went down and never went out the front  door for the next two weeks the 18,000  were gone in two days and I think the  statistics now are fairly well known  that there overall there were 400,000  distributed and we received well over  20,000 letters from all but about two or  three states what is not so well known  as this and this is very important for  you reporters who might want to go back  and talk to editors who might be  frustrated marketing executives menu  masquerading as editors that the  circulation of the paper for nine days  went up every single day as many as  10,000 a day at the end of the series it  dropped back down but we did receive  many letters from people who said they  had not subscribed the paper before but  did so now and letters from a couple  that said we bought three copies each  day and gave the two other copies of the  series to friends there was one major  disappointment from this series of Bill  Moyers did two one-hour programs based  on an adaptation this reason when we  were going through this process we  thought wow this is it this is a big  meal ticket to the really good money of  the electronic media we're going to be  out of here  and everything was really going well  until the day the first program aired  and now we know what it's like to be  savaged by the news media and it was bad  enough we were savaged but their own  paper The Enquirer TV critic reviewed it  and said everybody should watch these  programs that are really great and then  he did this truly malicious thing and I  want to read this so get the words  exactly right he says because we got to  remember now we forget we're going to be  the Siskel and Ebert of economic news he  says he says on the TV personality scale  Bartlett and steel are so laid back is  to be almost completely horizontal and  then I mean we never did anything to  this guy as far as I know but then he  got really personal he says at one point  Bartlett gets so outraged that he  actually arches an eyebrow and then he  concluded by saying the two of them are  Clarke Kennish enough to make Bill  Moyers look like Morton Downey jr. and  at that point we sent a letter to a  Moyers asking if you'd like to join in  litigation but one of our lawyer friends  pointed out the truth is still an  absolute defense so wised against a  gentle talk a little bit more about the  response  want to talk a little bit about the  reaction to the series and now the  subsequent reaction now that the book is  out there because it we think it says a  lot for our profession and also has a  great deal to say about the American  public as well don alluded to these  twenty thousand letters that we received  there was a certain quality to the  response both in the letters and in the  phone calls that we received right after  the original series he had an  astonishing volume of this mail began  coming in phone lines were jamming The  Inquirer switchboard some of the letters  and requests came in by the most bizarre  manner some were written on the back of  wine labels from the Napa Valley in  California one request for reprints was  scribbled on the back of the marching  song on Wisconsin one day after the  series originally appeared a frantic  caller came called from the fella  Manning the switchboard thinks it was  somewhere in the Midwest and said he had  heard by word of mouth that there was  this series that The Inquirer had done  on the American economy by two fellows  by the name of Bartles and Jaymes  this good natured flirt later sent him a  copy of the reprint and he says he threw  in a 6-pack of a peach wine cooler as  well wherever that gentleman is we will  thank him for his support you know what  what accounts for this reaction I mean  we keep being asked that and we