sorb  numbers and listen to hard facts and  respond to them frankly we never had the  documentary evidence like we did as a  result of this series with these 20,000  letters and these letters are an  astonishing outpouring from the American  people all over the country all walks of  life rich poor and as Don said black  white Hispanic no simple way to describe  them they show very much the hopes the  dreams the concerns that so many people  have for the country and I think most  interesting of all to us their optimism  and in closing I'd like to read you just  the comments of one woman from  Pennsylvania who wrote us about America  what went wrong and what she said was  very symbolic of what literally hundreds  of others wrote us after thanking the  newspaper for investing the time and the  effort she said quite simply there are a  lot of good people who care and who want  to do something to turn this situation  around most of them are mute you to have  opened a Pandora's box of trouble and  grief that the heavies have been sitting  on for a long time  but I think you have pinched the  conscience of the silent majority and  hopefully something very positive will  result thank you so much we'd be happy  to answer some questions that you may  have  thank you for those remarks a questioner  wants to know what is the state of  investigative reporting today 20 years  after Watergate this is a question we  are constantly especially from younger  people coming into the business because  they get a lot of gloom and doom out  there and since I'm older I can tell  people that the gloom and doom is not  warranted the reason there is a there's  a sense that investigative reporting is  not as as great or as prevalent as it  once was is based on what people are  looking at in metropolitan newspapers  and metropolitan newspapers have never  ever been a gauge of the state of  investigative reporting in this country  there is probably not probably there is  more investigative reporting on medium  size and small daily newspapers going on  in this country now and at any time in  this nation's history and if you attend  a convention like Ayari or other  conventions where you see this work the  sad fact is that this these projects are  never pulled together in one place where  we can see them we never know about them  until you go out to a convention or or  hear someone someone call you and send  you one the volume of this work and the  quality of it is mind-boggling  and if it really should put most  metropolitan newspapers to shame they  should be embarrassed by it because the  observations that are being made on the  on the health of investigative reporting  really are based on what metropolitan  newspapers are doing but they've never  they never historically did that much to  begin with  so we think that basically the future is  very very bright  what specific training do you recommend  for young reporters for example  accounting or research methods one of  the nice things about doing something  like this you never know what kind of  questions you're going to be hit by  there's this is an interesting debate in  our profession about specialized  training and while I don't think either  one of us would ever discourage any type  of specialized training in a way we can  almost go too far in certain areas but I  think for young reporters I think Don  and I are both pretty much in agreement  on this rather than trying to foresee  and conceive your specialty early on you  should still get a broad-based training  in your field whether it's radio TV the  print media because only by seeing how  government works at all its levels to  get a full appreciation of how our  system as a whole operates the two of us  spent years laboring and what a famous  composer once described as the galleys  referring to all of it that hard work  you do day in and day out to churn out  stories we both spent years and years  doing daily stories of a wide variety on  a wide variety of subjects had nothing  to do with long-range reporting had  nothing to do with enterprise reporting  and I am most thankful for all the time  I spent doing that because I learned how  local school boards operate I've seen  how local City Councils operate I've  seen how what local political races are  like at the grassroots level you see and  absorb an appreciation of democracy  that's invaluable to your work at  whatever level you may pursue it so we  are firm believers and starting at small  medium sized papers definitely not at  large institutions if possible  we have two questions on the same theme  now that you know what went wrong to you  since the nation is turning the  situation around and your book documents  tremendous job losses due to mergers and  acquisitions and deregulation is this  still happening and where let me try  there let's see these are the book  documents tremendous job losses these  are continuing to happen I mean in  deregulation alone when Trans World  Airlines and America West and  Continental all entered bankruptcy some  time back everybody assumed that they  would all emerge at some point the  stories are becoming increasingly gloomy  for those airlines if not emerging at  least in some reduced fashion from what  they used to be what we see we see a  continuing fallout through the 1990s  largely because of some of the excesses  that were built up in the 1980s when you  look at the size of the corporate debt  loads that so many companies took on in  the 1980s we're going to be paying that  price for a long time it's even it's  hitting companies that otherwise are  very solid companies Macy's is probably  an excellent example of that Macy's is  basically from Olin what anyone can see  a very solid company but because of the  excessive debt that it took on in the  1980s as part of that gogo philosophy as  to why debt was good and made companies  cleaner and leaner and meaner and so  forth Macy's is now in bankruptcy and  starting 