t better  that may not be true but it tends to  look that way on the other hand I think  we're both very optimistic about the  American people and you can't help when  you spend as much time as we did going  around the country and one of the fun  things and the fascinating things not  necessarily fun but fascinating and very  instructive things from the book is  we've done about no more than 75 radio  talk shows now and half the states in  the country technology is such that  unfortunately we never have to leave the  Inquirer to do this we just sit in the  office rather than getting to travel but  the questions that come in in these I  mean we've been in the business long  enough as you people many of you people  have you can sort out the whack jobs of  very very quickly you know when the  questioner is a serious person and you  know when you're dealing with someone  who is really very concerned and this is  across the board it doesn't matter  whether it's a small town as we did a  radio station in Streeter Illinois that  had the mayor on along with the talk  show host or or Michael Jackson in Los  Angeles or or any of the programs in  Boston in New York the questions that  are coming in there are very similar to  what we found when we went out and did  the interviews around the country that  produced a series people are concerned  and they want to see something done and  clearly what's happening now is they're  casting about trying to find out what  the best way is to do that  I want to preface this story with a this  question with a story that mr. bralette  told me he said that his editors don't  like to ask them any questions about  their their work because they'll say  that's a very good question  and they'll come back two days later and  the story will be 90 inches longer the  question is did your editors cut any  good stuff  there's a funny story that max King  tells about this it's probably the best  one and that is in the original series  was picked up by roughly 50 newspapers  around the country at least that we know  of some picked up one part some picked  up two or three parts some ran actually  all nine parts but reduce them somewhat  and at one gathering we once we once  made the point that none of them of  course ran the series in entirety to  which max jumped up and said nobody ran  it in entirety including the  Philadelphia Enquirer like all of us  there were many good parts that  obviously you have to go through a  synthesis process and some of this  actually found their way back into the  book we also received many wonderful  tips from people around the country some  wonderful ideas about things to expand  on that we were unaware of so the book  also served as a mini follow-up to some  parts of the series so yes there's  always a synthesis process that that  goes through but nothing of real great  substance that in any way reduced the  impact or the significance of what we  felt the story was about was was left  out  the one questioner wants to know to what  degree do you connect the rise of Ross  Pro to your series or maybe another way  of asking this is are you seeing your  work recycled in the rhetoric of the  political campaign this year the Ross  Perot wins an interesting when sitting  in the office a few weeks after the  series ran and the phone rings and I  pick it up and he says I say Bartlett  and he says Don he said this is Ross  Perot and I said right  figured it was some friend but it really  was him and it's clear now when you look  back all those months what was happening  at the time because he said what he  wanted to do with the reprints was put  them into a restaurant that he goes to  every day not far from his home or a  couple of days a week which is basically  a restaurant that attracts a solid  middle-class group of people blue collar  white collar and he wanted to gauge the  reaction of the people who read the  reprint in the restaurant that was back  in November I don't think there's any  question you see large parts of it  coming out I think one of the we haven't  seen Governor Clinton's the full  proposal but just what we read in the  newspaper the tax part of it clearly  comes right out of the book when he's  talking about foreign corporations not  paying a share of taxes that they should  pay in this country and when he talks  about raising taxes at the top end of  the scale most of the tax policy issues  look like they came out of the book and  and other issues as well one last thing  I might add part of the appeal he's  having too is clearly a part of a  widespread disenchantment that we're  seeing around the country I'm sure many  of you are as well with both both  parties and there's no doubt that that  has to do with a lot of why he's getting  response he is people are so fed up  rightly or wrongly with what they  perceived to be the way both parties  have handled economic issues in  and the way government is operated in  Washington that's probably the single  most widespread reason for a lot of this  appeal a questioner asked in this age of  databases and power journalism what  resources did you rely on for your  information a library a computer your  car good question we used a lot of  databases electronic databases and we  have for years on a whole wide range of  things we used an electronic database  several years ago to decode one of the  special tax breaks and the tax reform  activator that was proposed after the  tax reform act of 86  so databases represent a wonderful  resource out there but most the most  significant probably database we used  was one we created ourselves rather than  buying material from somebody else we  simply one of the most exciting things  we do is to input data on a computer or  PC and all the charts in the book and  the series before that which would  capture the attention of so many people  were based on basically a very tedious  painstaking process I give an example 1  shows salaries and wages of people by  income groups in the 1980s for example  it's a very dramatic chart that shows  the increase of people earning more than  1 million what their cumulative  increases were versus people 20 to  50,000 the heart of the middle-class  very small increase that's based on ten  years of statistics of income from the  Internal Revenue Service or each one of  those reports is xeroxed or obtained  from the IRS and the particular numbers  input in a computer and then analyzed  some of the data we went back to the 30s  and 40s analyzing corporate payments and  tax collections each one input a  year-by-year  exciting stuff right  but tedious work but absolutely at the  heart of what you ultimately end up with  which is a very dramatic chart and  example so the computer in that sense  has become a tremendously important tool  for us other than that our techniques by  which we organize material and store it  and keep track of it are basically very  old-fashioned we don't have  sophisticated filing systems we file  information simply by subject matter  company individual general subject area  the single most valuable tool we know of  is the oldest tool that exists in  civilization and that is to reread the  material how many times do you  transcribe an interview or watch  something you've seen before and there's  something new in it the same thing is  true of a written document a written  report you see that over and over again  the mind can only focus on so many  things so we try to basically read and  reread our own material whether it's  transcribed interviews government  reports findings from databases and that  is served us better than any single  technique over the years is there an  advantage to having a dual investigative  team I think the the advantage is  obviously going to do twice as much work  the other real advantage and we see this  a lot of times with people working on  their first project or two is when two  people are working together you can  bounce things off 