 doing daily  reporting and we just find this much  more fascinating much more interesting  several questions just arrived on the  series and the economics of it in view  of the strength of foreign competition  was the restructuring of the US economy  avoidable or is what you've documented  primarily the result of the Reagan  Republican policies of the Reagan Bush  administration's I think the first thing  to say anyone who reads the book in the  series before that realizes that the  Democrats don't come out looking very  good in this either I mean many of the  deregulation policies which have had  such an adverse effect on many  middle-class Americans date from the  late 70s and even many of the measures  enacted for the S&amp;L industry were pushed  by Democrats so it's been a joint  arrangement here that has led us to this  particular situation in terms of the  argument on the restructuring was this  restructuring avoidable or necessary if  you're talking about companies that were  ticketed for oblivion our industries  that were ticketed for oblivion that's  one thing but what we saw over and over  again in which we wrote about in the  story was we're not industries were not  factories that were necessarily  inefficient or unprofitable we've never  forgotten one in Missouri outside of st.  Louis where the restructuring which was  brought on but when a corporate raider  made a run at the company in the company  to buy off that the Raiders loaded up on  debt and when they could not manage the  debt they had to start selling assets  generating cash to pay down that debt  they had to do that in a hurry and one  of those plants that was closed right  away it was one of the most profitable  and well-run in the company simply  because the corporation owned the land  owned the machinery and could quickly  turn that around so you had from so much  of the restructuring not laying the  groundwork for a long-term improvement  in the in the country but almost an  exacerbation of this short-term  psychology that gripped the country so  much in the 1980s  do you get any sense that the  intellectual underpinnings behind the  regulation are eroded enough that that  could be changed in regulation some  deregulated industries brought back  under regulation both you can't turn the  clock back once things like this  happened but it's interesting to  contrast what's happening with the  Europeans now I know if anybody saw the  stories today about airline deregulation  in Europe if all you read were the  headlines you would think gee the  Europeans are going the same route that  we did in this country with the airlines  but and we haven't seen the actual  papers yet but if you look at if you  read some of the fine print in terms of  the airline deregulation in Europe it's  considerably different because the  national governments are all going to  have a hand and a say-so to make sure  not destructive price wars do not  develop and if you look at what's  happened in the US does it make any  sense to create and then run out of  business in a short number of years X  number of Airlines you never had an  airline bankruptcy in this country  before deregulation of the airline  industry in 1978 since then as everybody  knows six major carriers alone have gone  out of business many others are in  bankruptcy we're clearly moving toward a  concentration in the airline industry  unless no one denies that the original  idea of deregulation which was promoting  competition is not going to work so why  you can't turn that back probably the  most important thing is to watch the  future things like banking and other  industries to make sure many of the  mistakes made there in the past are not  repeated in the future  question-it wants to know what is the  sequel to this series I'm not sure we  want to answer that a room full of  reporters uh we got so many questions  during the series from people basically  turned around the issue of forum trade  exports and imports and this again goes  to the heart of really very thoughtful  people out there and we had a call from  one woman who this was right around  Christmas and she came back with his  entire Christmas list of things she had  bought and she said I want to know why  they're being made there I want to know  what the consequences were for the  people who used to make these products  here and the one that really got our  attention when she read around she said  I bought this bra and it says  manufactured in the Dominican Republic  from components made in the US and she  said could you explain that to me and I  said we'll find out  today the Press Club gave out a great  many journalism awards honoring and  recognizing the work of journalists from  around the country have the awards that  you won have they motivated you in your  work does journalism need more or fewer  awards and contests I think the greatest  motivation we get is from readers and  that was that's why this series in  particular was so important to us  because when you spend two years in a  project we had no idea what was going to  happen when max King we asked max the  The Inquirer had never before been a  series more than seven parts never  carried over into a second week and when  we made the case with max we wanted to  go nine parts so we're going into two  weeks this was something had never been  done before and he he said I want to  think about it overnight came back the  next day and said we'll do it  everybody was rolling the dice in this  series in a way not only in terms of  length in in the word count but in the  number of parts was the question  oh the prizes now it's it's really the  response of people interestingly  jean-robert's who was so responsible for  carving out the enquires tradition has a  theory especially in terms of us he  doesn't believe that our best series  ever won the major awards and he usually  cites our energy series nuclear waste  and the very earlier when we did was a  computerized study of the violent crime  in Philadelphia it was the first time a  computer had ever been used on that  scale by a newspaper and phil meyer did  the programming for us we actually  leased the computer down here out in  maryland at the navy used to track it  ships that's a large the computer was at  that time he had to punch all the  information and i-iv em cards when that  was in urge for a Pulitzer had never  made it past the editor who said he  didn't think the newspaper should ever  use computers and I think that's why you  never ever ever do things for prize as  you do them because there is a there's a  need to inform people and you're looking  for the response from readers not  anything else  because you spend as much time as anyone  rummaging around the public record  looking for information I wonder if you  could tell us what the state is of open  records laws today and specifically a  question from someone here are you  disturbed by the control private firms  designated by the federal government  have over computerized government  records not sure we can answer this with  a great deal of authority only because  the problem with records it is a  continuing one in every agency at all  times we have been the biggest obstacle  we have been confronted over the years  is not so much the access to records but  is finding the records and then trying  to make sense of them and use of them  once once you you find them because  we've used heavily public records public  documents over over time and people are  under the misunderstanding that because  we use them we know where everything is  that there's some magical book or  process that lets you know exactly where  you can go it's called knowing where to  look and people ask us this all the time  well we never know where to look unless  we're going back to exactly the same  kind of record or document that we have  used in the past  so this best advice we can give to  anybody dealing with records at all  levels of government is a very simple  one so many clerks and bureaucracies  whether they be local or state or  federal are very orderly in their  behavior and their activities they like  to have clean desks and if you want  something that you think they have just  stick there with them you become an  obstacle to the efficient operation of  their enterprise and they've been  amazing to us how many times when  somebody says something isn't available  something is not there then when you  continue to asking what is this based on  what is the regulation what is the law  politely nicely how eventually they come  around to your way of thinking say yes I  checked it is in fact available go do it  but there's no simple solution for  getting around that other than to ask  people specific  what it is that they're using to deny  you that information before I ask the  final question I'd like to present you  both with certificates of appreciation  from the National Press Club and  something you'll find handy back in  Philadelphia the official National Press  Club coffee mug it really does work  questioner says you took a swipe at  editors who you really think or  marketeers could you please elaborate on  that at length gee we can do this for  the next 24 hours we're talking about  people who say that the editor people  are editors who say that people don't  want to read stories or more than 18  inches long or 10 inches long they don't  want to read a story off the jump  there's one it's always hung out there  the president of a large metropolitan  newspaper sent out a memo saying that  words to that effect that people didn't  want to read long stories and they  especially did not want to read the  traditionally dull boring government  stories that newspapers have always  covered and our response basically to  that is there are no dull boring stories  they're only dull boring reporters  editors and most importantly publishers  who don't recognize the difference out  there and earn trying to to create a  product that people really don't want it  and I'll give you another example here  on these radio talk shows and we've also  talked I don't know two dozen audiences  around the country League of Women  Voters groups like that people who are  clearly sophisticated people who read  and every single place we have talked  the question has been raised what is  wrong with the news media why are  newspapers radio television covering the  issues that affect our lives and we have  an answer to that and it is basically  that this marketing people are tell  editor's or the editors are believing  them that people don't want information  anymore nothing really has ever changed  in this business it was back in in the  early 1930s when Stanley Walker was the  legendary editor of the New York Herald  Tribune said very simply facts sell  newspapers nothing has changed since  then except the perception on some part  of some people that maybe it isn't  working anymore and that's been really  the most rewarding thing about this  series because at least it has caught  the attention of a few editors who said  gee maybe people really do want to read  I'd like to thank you for coming to  today's luncheon once in and again  congratulations to all the Award winners  here today and thanks also to the judges  who helped find our Award winners thank  you all for coming
 

